WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.435
-Hey, buddy.
Nice to see you.

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.903
Thank you for coming back
on the show.

00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:06.772
-Thank you for having me back.
It's like almost what?

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:08.708
A year since last time I think?

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:10.576
-That's the trophy
right behind you?

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:12.979
-Yeah, there it is,
that's the bad boy.

00:00:12.979 --> 00:00:15.414
-Congrats, bud. This is
the second year in a row

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:18.384
that your team won the
Overwatch League Grand Finals.

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:21.354
Could you first quickly explain
what Overwatch is for anyone

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.355
who doesn't know?

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.423
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:26.425
Basically Overwatch,
it's 6 versus 6.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:29.462
a team-based action game by
Blizzard Entertainment.

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:30.963
The goal -- you've got different
maps,

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:33.266
you got different objectives on
these different maps,

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:35.034
you play with
a wide range of heroes,

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.769
they all have different
abilities,

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:40.340
and you work together and you
complete the objectives.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.975
-Well, this year, the win --

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.343
obviously this year's
a little different.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:46.579
Last time we talked you were in
the Wells Fargo Center

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:47.980
in front of 12,000 people.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.183
This is a photo right after
you won. This is last year.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:51.584
Look at that.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:55.221
-And now this is a photo of what
winning looks like in 2020.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.455
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:59.625
-Still exciting but a different
scene, huh?

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:02.495
-Right, yeah, I mean, last year
it was super exciting because,

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:03.863
you know, I'm from Philadelphia,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.799
Wells Fargo Center,
I've been there many times.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:09.935
I saw Flyers games there
a ton as a kid.

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:12.872
This year, still feels pretty
good, but it's a lot different.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:17.710
There's no crowds, there's no
excitement in the big arenas,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.145
the sold-out arenas.

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.479
It was actually kind of funny
'cause I remember

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.282
a lot of people were curious
'cause, like, the rooms

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:24.884
that we played in were so small

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.253
that, like, I couldn't be
with the rest of my team

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.988
when they were playing,
but, you know,

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.624
it still feels good --
feels good to win again.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.192
-That's great, bud.

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:36.395
Gamers all have nicknames,

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:38.832
and you go by the nickname
"Super."

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:39.766
-Yes.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.601
-Wow. Okay, how did that --

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:45.003
Where did that come about?
'Cause you're that good?

00:01:45.003 --> 00:01:49.775
-Well, actually, it was 'cause
when I was a lot younger,

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:51.544
my brother --
I watched him play a game.

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.245
It was called Runescape,
way back in the day.

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:54.681
I was, like, a little kid.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.917
And I wanted to play but I
was -- what? -- four years old?

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.553
I didn't know how to set up an
account or anything like that,

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.456
so I asked him to help me, and
the name that he came up with --

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.857
I don't know
how he got this name.

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:07.426
It was Super71000.

00:02:07.426 --> 00:02:09.429
And that's what I stuck with
for a while.

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:13.065
But then at some point I just
decided the numbers look stupid,

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:16.168
so I cut off the numbers and
then I just was stuck with super

00:02:16.168 --> 00:02:18.104
and I just -- I just kept it.

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:20.139
-Is there a formula
to come up --

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:22.642
How could I come up with
my gamer name?

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.877
-You find inspiration for
literally anything.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:27.346
-Okay, I could do...

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:31.384
This is the Little Empire State.
I could be "Empire." Empire.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.818
-There you go.

00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:35.788
-Or this is
the Statue of Liberty,

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.923
but it's an old thermometer.

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:40.859
Maybe "Old Thermometer."

00:02:40.859 --> 00:02:42.194
That's my gamer name.

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:44.797
-Hey, listen, if you're feeling
it, that's all you.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.632
-Jimmy "Old Thermometer" Fallon.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:50.536
"And a come-from-behind win
from 'Old Thermometer!'"

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:53.206
Now that you have two
championships under your belt,

00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.074
the big question people
want to know.

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.409
Are you planning on going for
the three-peat?

00:02:57.409 --> 00:03:02.014
-Yeah, I mean, before playoffs,
actually, our general manager,

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:04.684
he made us watch "The Last
Dance" documentary as a team.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:06.318
So, you know, Chicago Bulls,

00:03:06.318 --> 00:03:08.388
Michael Jordan,
that whole ordeal.

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:10.589
And, you know, for me, it added,
like, motivation to just

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:12.392
kind of, like, go out there
and do it again

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:15.628
because I feel like e-sports
and, like, professional gaming

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:21.400
is still so young that I have
a unique position to be

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.970
one of the first dynasties
in e-sports.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.305
I think that's really exciting
and really fun to kind of, like,

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:29.042
cement yourself in that domain.

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:31.777
But, you know, I don't want to
think too far in the future

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:33.079
because then you
overthink things

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.147
and you get all anxious
and nervous.

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:37.817
I just take it,
you know, day by day.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.551
We'll see where the wind goes.

00:03:39.551 --> 00:03:41.553
-I think you're already
in the history books, buddy.

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:43.456
Congrats.
-Thank you.

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.892
-Matthew "Super" Delisi,
everyone.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.594
Congratulations again, pal.

